## 4. The auth engine

One authentication engine for all roles — the login path differs per role (`/admin/login`, `/teacher/login`, `/student/login`, `/parent/login`), but they all hit the **same** backend auth, which then resolves the user's roles and permissions. The database decides what each user can see, not the URL.

### 4.1 Custom User model

Do **not** use Django's default username-based user. Login is by email (staff/admin) or a school-issued identifier (students by admission number, parents by phone/email). A single `User` table with a `user_type` discriminator keeps auth unified while profile tables hold role-specific data.

```python
# apps/accounts/models.py
from django.contrib.auth.models import AbstractBaseUser, PermissionsMixin
from django.db import models
from common.models import BaseModel

class User(AbstractBaseUser, PermissionsMixin, BaseModel):
    class UserType(models.TextChoices):
        STAFF = "staff"       # admin, principal, teacher, accountant, HR, etc.
        STUDENT = "student"
        PARENT = "parent"
        APPLICANT = "applicant"

    email = models.EmailField(unique=True, null=True, blank=True)
    phone = models.CharField(max_length=20, unique=True, null=True, blank=True)
    # identifier is the login handle when not email — e.g. admission number
    identifier = models.CharField(max_length=50, unique=True, null=True, blank=True)

    user_type = models.CharField(max_length=20, choices=UserType.choices)
    full_name = models.CharField(max_length=150)
    avatar = models.URLField(null=True, blank=True)

    is_active = models.BooleanField(default=True)
    is_staff = models.BooleanField(default=False)   # Django admin access only
    is_superadmin = models.BooleanField(default=False)  # bypasses RBAC checks

    two_factor_enabled = models.BooleanField(default=False)
    last_login_at = models.DateTimeField(null=True, blank=True)

    USERNAME_FIELD = "email"          # primary; managers handle alt logins
    REQUIRED_FIELDS = ["full_name"]

    objects = UserManager()
```

> **Note on `is_superadmin`:** the Super Administrator bypasses granular permission checks entirely — it is the one role that always returns `True` from `has_permission`. Every other role, including Principal, is governed by explicit permission strings.

### 4.2 Login flow (with optional 2FA)

```
POST /api/v1/auth/login
    { "login": "principal@mountcarmel.edu", "password": "..." }
        │
        ├── validate credentials (constant-time, hashed compare)
        ├── if user.two_factor_enabled:
        │       → generate 6-digit OTP, store hashed w/ 5-min expiry
        │       → dispatch OTP via Celery (email/SMS)
        │       → return { "requires_2fa": true, "challenge_id": "..." }
        │
        └── else:
                → issue access + refresh JWT
                → write LoginHistory row (ip, user-agent, success)
                → return tokens + user profile + resolved permissions[]

POST /api/v1/auth/verify-otp
    { "challenge_id": "...", "code": "123456" }
        → on success issue JWT (same as above)
```

**JWT contents (access token claims):**

```json
{
  "sub": "<user_uuid>",
  "user_type": "staff",
  "is_superadmin": false,
  "roles": ["principal"],
  "perms_version": 42,        // bumped when a role's perms change → forces re-fetch
  "exp": 1735689600
}
```

> Don't stuff the full permission list into the JWT (it can be large and goes stale). Put a `perms_version` in the token and resolve the actual permission set server-side per request (cached in Redis). When an admin edits a role's permissions, bump the version so clients re-authenticate their permission view.

